# Performance requirements

Read this before changing background work, synchronization, storage, caches or UI
state. Mesh Talk should cost little while waiting, load data when needed and stay
bounded as history grows. This guide owns cross-cutting performance requirements;
[module ownership](module-boundaries.md), [network budgets](network-design-reference.md#8-resource-budgets-compatibility-and-performance)
and [reliability rules](ai-coding-reliability.md) own their implementation contracts.

## Budgets and measurement

These are initial project targets, **not industry standards or claims that the
current application meets them**. Establish a production-build baseline on each
supported platform. Reference fixture: 4 logical CPUs, 8 GiB RAM, SSD, 100 contacts,
20 groups and 100,000 stored messages; no pending transfers, calls or animations.
Separate cold start, warm start, catch-up, visible idle and hidden idle. Measure a
quiet five-minute window after warmup; record build, runtime, profile and workload.
Warm startup restarts the process with OS caches retained; cold startup records
its uncached/first-launch conditions. Usable means an actionable list or
conversation, not a blank shell; report human authorization waits separately.
The empty-shell comparison keeps the same backend, runtime, process membership
and window settings, with user payloads absent.

| Resource                | Initial target for the reference fixture                                                                                                                                                            |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Idle CPU                | Visible desktop: ≤1% of one logical CPU; hidden desktop: ≤0.25%; headless node: ≤0.5%, averaged over the window.                                                                                    |
| Retained memory         | Headless node: ≤64 MiB private commit. Desktop: ≤64 MiB workload delta above the same-build empty shell; establish a reviewed whole-family ceiling from that baseline.                              |
| Memory growth           | Repeated navigation/media cycles reach a bounded plateau. Tenfold stored history must not require tenfold startup reads or retained UI payloads.                                                    |
| Idle writes             | Zero business-data commits for unchanged inputs. Target ≤5 MiB/hour OS-observed attributable storage writes per application family after warmup; measure the full hour before claiming this target. |
| Background wakeups      | No application maintenance timer without pending work, a real deadline or a documented recovery purpose. Noncritical recovery defaults to ≥5 minutes and coalesces activation.                      |
| Startup and interaction | First usable shell ≤1 s warm / ≤3 s cold; ordinary interaction must not run blocking I/O, crypto or an unbounded traversal on the UI thread. Report hydration/catch-up separately.                  |

CPU means `100 × process CPU seconds / wall seconds`, summed over the full app
family; divide by logical CPU count only when reporting whole-machine percent.
Report Rust host **and** renderer/GPU/helpers. Distinguish private commit, private
resident memory, shared pages and live JS heap; on Linux use PSS for shared-page
attribution. Logical I/O includes IPC/network activity and is not physical disk
I/O; OS disk events also do not measure physical NAND write amplification. CPU alone does not establish battery/power efficiency: inspect wakeups, GPU,
network and actual energy where supported. Keep metrics collected in different
windows separate. Store measurements in PR/CI artifacts, not this guide.

### Desktop acceptance

Use the desktop CPU, retained-memory and startup figures above as optimization
targets. A desktop change can be accepted above those figures when affected
functional and recovery checks pass, there is no known blocking defect, and
comparable measurements show no material regression against the platform's
established baseline. Record the build, measurement conditions, observed gaps
and any missing platform evidence in the PR or CI evidence.

Treat reproducible freezes, data loss, broken recovery, unbounded growth and
repeated work on unchanged inputs as defects. Investigate a sustained resource
regression and attribute it before accepting a baseline change. Keep headless
and post-office budgets in force. Migrations, catch-up and active media have
their own bounded work budgets and progress evidence.

//! Durable local disclosure permissions. Hosts serialize access to this store;
//! public admission for a verified account ID is
//! `!snapshot.invisible || policy.allows(account_id)`.
use crate::storage::encryption::{
    EncryptionKey, NONCE_SIZE, SALT_SIZE, decrypt_data, encrypt_data, generate_salt,
};
use bincode::Options;
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use std::{
    fs::{self, File},
    io::{self, Read, Write},
    path::{Path, PathBuf},
};

const MAGIC: &[u8; 6] = b"MTPRV1";
const MAX_BYTES: u64 = 1_048_576;
const HEADER: usize = MAGIC.len() + SALT_SIZE + NONCE_SIZE;

fn invalid() -> io::Error {
    io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::InvalidData, "invalid privacy policy")
}
fn crypto_error(_: impl std::fmt::Display) -> io::Error {
    invalid()
}
fn valid_id(id: &str) -> bool {
    id.len() == 32
        && id
            .bytes()
            .all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit() || (b'a'..=b'f').contains(&b))
}
fn codec() -> impl Options {
    bincode::DefaultOptions::new()
        .with_fixint_encoding()
        .with_limit(MAX_BYTES)
        .reject_trailing_bytes()
}
fn check_path(path: &Path) -> io::Result<bool> {
    match fs::symlink_metadata(path) {
        Ok(m) if m.is_file() => Ok(true),
        Ok(_) => Err(invalid()),
        Err(e) if e.kind() == io::ErrorKind::NotFound => Ok(false),
        Err(e) => Err(e),
    }
}
fn parent(path: &Path) -> &Path {
    path.parent()
        .filter(|p| !p.as_os_str().is_empty())
        .unwrap_or(Path::new("."))
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub enum PermissionSource {
    Manual,
    Initiated,
}
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct AllowedAccount {
    pub id: String,
    pub name: String,
    pub source: PermissionSource,
}
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct PrivacySnapshot {
    pub version: u32,
    pub invisible: bool,
    pub allowed_accounts: Vec<AllowedAccount>,
}
/// One encrypted policy file; mutations commit to disk before changing memory.
///
/// The host must maintain one live owner per file and serialize access: this
/// store has no cross-process locking. Its parent directory must be trusted;
/// path checks reject existing symlinks but do not defend against filesystem
/// changes racing those checks. Admission decisions require account IDs verified
/// by the host's authentication layer.
pub struct PrivacyPolicy {
    path: PathBuf,
    salt: [u8; SALT_SIZE],
    key: EncryptionKey,
    snapshot: PrivacySnapshot,
}
impl PrivacyPolicy {
    /// Open encrypted policy or create a public default; malformed data fails closed.
    /// The caller must serialize ownership and keep the parent directory trusted.
    pub fn open(path: &Path, password: &str) -> io::Result<Self> {
        if !check_path(path)? {
            let salt = generate_salt();
            let p = Self {
                path: path.into(),
                salt,
                key: EncryptionKey::from_password(password, &salt).map_err(crypto_error)?,
                snapshot: PrivacySnapshot {
                    version: 1,
                    invisible: false,
                    allowed_accounts: Vec::new(),
                },
            };
            p.save(&p.snapshot)?;
            return Ok(p);
        }
        let mut bytes = Vec::new();
        let file = File::open(path)?;
        if !file.metadata()?.is_file() {
            return Err(invalid());
        }
        file.take(MAX_BYTES + 1).read_to_end(&mut bytes)?;
        if bytes.len() < HEADER + 16
            || bytes.len() as u64 > MAX_BYTES
            || &bytes[..MAGIC.len()] != MAGIC
        {
            return Err(invalid());
        }
        let salt = bytes[MAGIC.len()..MAGIC.len() + SALT_SIZE]
            .try_into()
            .map_err(crypto_error)?;
        let nonce = bytes[MAGIC.len() + SALT_SIZE..HEADER]
            .try_into()
            .map_err(crypto_error)?;
        let key = EncryptionKey::from_password(password, &salt).map_err(crypto_error)?;
        let plaintext = decrypt_data(&bytes[HEADER..], &nonce, &key).map_err(crypto_error)?;
        let snapshot: PrivacySnapshot = codec().deserialize(&plaintext).map_err(crypto_error)?;
        if snapshot.version != 1 || snapshot.allowed_accounts.len() > 1024 {
            return Err(invalid());
        }
        let mut previous: Option<&str> = None;
        for account in &snapshot.allowed_accounts {
            if !valid_id(&account.id)
                || previous.is_some_and(|p| p >= account.id.as_str())
                || account.name.chars().count() > 256
                || account.name.chars().any(char::is_control)
            {
                return Err(invalid());
            }
            previous = Some(&account.id);
        }
        Ok(Self {
            path: path.into(),
            salt,
            key,
            snapshot,
        })
    }
    /// Return a detached snapshot for the authorized local settings UI.
    pub fn snapshot(&self) -> PrivacySnapshot {
        self.snapshot.clone()
    }
    /// Returns allowlist membership independently of the current visibility mode.
    pub fn allows(&self, id: &str) -> bool {
        valid_id(id)
            && self
                .snapshot
                .allowed_accounts
                .binary_search_by(|a| a.id.as_str().cmp(id))
                .is_ok()
    }
    /// Commit visibility before replacing memory; failed writes preserve prior state.
    pub fn set_invisible(&mut self, invisible: bool) -> io::Result<()> {
        let mut next = self.snapshot.clone();
        next.invisible = invisible;
        self.commit(next)
    }
    /// Persist a validated account grant, retaining sorted uniqueness and Manual priority.
    pub fn grant(&mut self, id: &str, name: &str, source: PermissionSource) -> io::Result<()> {
        if !valid_id(id) {
            return Err(invalid());
        }
        let name: String = name.chars().filter(|c| !c.is_control()).take(256).collect();
        let mut next = self.snapshot.clone();
        match next
            .allowed_accounts
            .binary_search_by(|a| a.id.as_str().cmp(id))
        {
            Ok(i) => {
                let a = &mut next.allowed_accounts[i];
                if !name.is_empty() {
                    a.name = name;
                }
                if source == PermissionSource::Manual {
                    a.source = source;
                }
            }
            Err(_) => {
                if next.allowed_accounts.len() == 1024 {
                    return Err(invalid());
                }
                next.allowed_accounts.push(AllowedAccount {
                    id: id.into(),
                    name,
                    source,
                });
                next.allowed_accounts
                    .sort_unstable_by(|a, b| a.id.cmp(&b.id));
            }
        }
        self.commit(next)
    }
    /// Persist removal of this account; revoking an absent valid ID is a no-op.
    pub fn revoke(&mut self, id: &str) -> io::Result<()> {
        if !valid_id(id) {
            return Err(invalid());
        }
        let mut next = self.snapshot.clone();
        next.allowed_accounts.retain(|account| account.id != id);
        self.commit(next)
    }
    fn commit(&mut self, next: PrivacySnapshot) -> io::Result<()> {
        if next == self.snapshot {
            return Ok(());
        }
        self.save(&next)?;
        self.snapshot = next;
        Ok(())
    }
    fn save(&self, snapshot: &PrivacySnapshot) -> io::Result<()> {
        let plaintext = codec().serialize(snapshot).map_err(crypto_error)?;
        let (ciphertext, nonce) = encrypt_data(&plaintext, &self.key).map_err(crypto_error)?;
        if HEADER + ciphertext.len() > MAX_BYTES as usize {
            return Err(invalid());
        }
        check_path(&self.path)?;
        let directory = parent(&self.path);
        let mut tmp = tempfile::NamedTempFile::new_in(directory)?;
        #[cfg(unix)]
        {
            use std::os::unix::fs::PermissionsExt;
            tmp.as_file()
                .set_permissions(fs::Permissions::from_mode(0o600))?;
        }
        tmp.write_all(MAGIC)?;
        tmp.write_all(&self.salt)?;
        tmp.write_all(&nonce)?;
        tmp.write_all(&ciphertext)?;
        tmp.as_file().sync_all()?;
        check_path(&self.path)?;
        tmp.persist(&self.path).map_err(|e| e.error)?;
        // Replacement is the commit point. A directory-sync failure must not
        // report rollback after the new file has already become visible.
        if let Ok(directory) = File::open(directory) {
            let _ = directory.sync_all();
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

